## Migration to .NET 8

### Critical Breaking Changes

| API | .NET 8 Behavior |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (since .NET 6) |

### Bug in the Original Code

**Race condition**: If two rapid `Enqueue` calls happen, only one `WaitOne` unblocks. The second item sits in the queue because `Reset()` clears the signal before the second `Dequeue` runs. Items are silently lost.

### Migrated Code — Using `Channel<T>`

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _processingTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _processingTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { /* expected on shutdown */ }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel is closed.");
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();          // signal no more items

        if (_cts is not null && _processingTask is not null)
        {
            // Give it time to drain, then force-cancel
            using var delayCts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
            try
            {
                await _processingTask.WaitAsync(delayCts.Token);
            }
            catch (TimeoutException)
            {
                await _cts.CancelAsync();    // replaces Thread.Abort()
                await _processingTask;
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

### Why Each Change Matters

| Old Pattern | New Pattern | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock` | `Channel<T>` | Thread-safe, backpressure-aware, no manual synchronization, fixes the race condition |
| `Thread` | `Task.Run` | Lighter; doesn't block a dedicated OS thread while awaiting |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Channel.Writer.Complete()` | Graceful drain — processes remaining items before stopping |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` | Cooperative cancellation; `Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| No cleanup | `IAsyncDisposable` | Deterministic resource cleanup |

### If You Need a Synchronous API (drop-in replacement)

```csharp
public void Stop()
{
    _channel.Writer.Complete();
    if (_processingTask is not null && !_processingTask.Wait(5000))
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();          // cooperative abort
        _processingTask.Wait();  // wait for cancellation to take effect
    }
    _cts?.Dispose();
}
```

### Other Pitfalls to Watch For

1. **`Handle()` exceptions** — An unhandled throw kills the loop. Wrap it in `try/catch` if `Handle` can fail per-item.
2. **Bounded channels** — If you switch to `CreateBounded<T>(capacity)`, `TryWrite` returns `false` when full. Use `WriteAsync` for backpressure.
3. **`ConfigureAwait(false)`** — Add it inside library code if you don't need a synchronization context.
